                  {
                    "a": "1850s: War of Independence (1857). 1880s: Indian National Congress founded (1885). 1900s: Partition of Bengal (1905); All India Muslim League founded (1906). 1910s: Lucknow Pact (1916); World War I (1914–1918). 1920s: Khilafat Movement (1922); Nehru Report (1928); Jinnah's Fourteen Points (1929). 1930s: Government of India Act (1935); elections of 1937; World War II begins (1939); Day of Deliverance (22 December 1939). 1940s: Pakistan Resolution (23 March 1940); elections of 1945–46; Pakistan came into being (14 August 1947).",
                    "q": "5. Make a time line to show the important events that occurred in each decade from 1857 to the independence of Pakistan in 1947."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "1930s: (1) The Government of India Act 1935 and the elections of 1937 — the Congress formed governments in 7 of the 11 provinces, made 'Vande Mataram' the national anthem, replaced Urdu with Hindi and banned cow slaughter, so the Muslims realised they needed a separate state. (2) World War II broke out in 1939; the Congress governments resigned and the Muslims observed 22 December 1939 as the Day of Deliverance. 1940s: (1) The Pakistan Resolution of 23 March 1940 demanded that the Muslim-majority areas be grouped into independent States. (2) In the elections of 1945–46 the Muslim League won all the Muslim seats in the Central Assembly, proving that the Muslims were united under Jinnah.",
                    "q": "6. Describe two important events in the 1930s and two important events in the 1940s that led to the creation of Pakistan."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "a) World War I (1914–1918): hundreds of thousands of Indian soldiers fought for Britain. In 1917 the British Prime Minister promised the Indians self-government if more soldiers fought. After the war, the Muslims started the Khilafat Movement (1922) because they feared the Allies would destroy the Caliphate in Turkey. b) World War II (1939–1945): Britain wanted the Indians to help fight; the Congress demanded the transfer of power, the British refused, and the Congress governments resigned in protest. The Muslims observed 22 December 1939 as the Day of Deliverance. The war's deaths and destruction led to the formation of the United Nations in 1945.",
                    "q": "7. State what effect each of the following world events had on the struggle for independence in the subcontinent: a) World War I. b) World War II."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "a) If the British did not come to India, the Mughal Empire might have continued, or the subcontinent might have been divided into many small states ruled by local rulers. b) If the Muslims did not form the All India Muslim League, there would have been no organised party to protect Muslim rights, and the Congress — a party of the Hindus — would have spoken for all Indians. c) If the British did not fight in World War II, the Congress governments might not have resigned, and the Day of Deliverance might not have been observed. d) If the Muslim League did not pass the Pakistan Resolution in 1940, there would have been no clear demand for a separate Muslim state, and Pakistan might not have come into being in 1947.",
                    "q": "8. State what you think might have happened: a) If the British did not come to India; b) If the Muslims did not form the All India Muslim League; c) If the British did not fight in World War II; d) If the Muslim League did not pass the Pakistan Resolution in 1940."
                  }
